hadiths[40]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1726","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""I will continue to fight against the people until they testify that there is no deity except Allah. When they do so, they will have protected their blood and their wealth from me, except for its due right, and their reckoning is with Allah."""
"1727","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I will continue to fight against the people until they testify that there is no deity except Allah. When they testify to this, they have protected their blood and wealth from me, except for its due right, and their reckoning is with Allah the Exalted."""
"1728","It is narrated from Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When Kisra dies, there will be no Kisra after him, and when Caesar dies, there will be no Caesar after him. By the One in Whose hand is my soul! You will spend the treasures of both of them (Caesar and Kisra) in the way of Allah."""
"1729","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that he said: When this verse was revealed: ""If there are twenty steadfast among you, they will overcome two hundred,"" it was made obligatory upon the Muslims that twenty men should not flee from facing two hundred. Then Allah the Exalted revealed: ""Now Allah has lightened your burden, and He knows that there is weakness in you. So if there are a hundred steadfast among you, they will overcome two hundred."" Thus, Allah lightened their burden and made it obligatory upon them that a hundred (Muslims) should not flee from facing two hundred (disbelievers)."
"1730","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that whoever (among Muslims) flees from facing three (disbelievers) has not truly fled, but whoever flees from facing two has truly fled."
"1731","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent us on a military expedition. When we encountered the enemy, the people fled. We came to Madinah, opened its gates, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)! We are those who fled (from the battlefield)."" So he (ﷺ) said: ""(No), rather you are those who returned to your leader for refuge, and I am the refuge of the Muslims."""
"1732","It is narrated from Buraidah (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would send out an army, he would appoint a commander over them and say: ""When you meet your polytheist enemies (on the battlefield), invite them to three things or three qualities. Alqamah was uncertain about these words. Invite them to accept Islam (first of all). If they accept your invitation, then accept it from them and withhold your sword from them. Then, if they accept Islam, invite them to leave their homeland and come to where the Muhajirun reside, and inform them that if they migrate, they will have the same benefits as the Muhajirun and the same obligations as the Muhajirun. If they choose to remain in their own place, they will be like the Bedouin Muslims; Allah's command will apply to them as it does to other Muslims, but they will have no share in the spoils of war (fay’) unless they fight alongside the Muslims (then they will have a share in the booty and fay’). If they refuse to accept this, then tell them to pay jizyah with their own hands in a state of humiliation. If they do this, accept it from them and leave them alone. But if they refuse even this, then seek help from Allah and fight them."""
"1733","It is narrated from Buraidah (may Allah be pleased with him) that whenever the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) would send out an army, he would appoint a commander over them... then he narrated a lengthy hadith."
"1734","Ibn Ka'b bin Malik narrated from his uncle that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he sent (people) to Ibn Abi Haqiq, forbade them from killing women and children."
"1735","Ibn Ka'b bin Malik narrated from his uncle that when the Prophet ﷺ sent (an army) towards Ibn Abi Haqiq, he forbade them from killing women and children."
"1736","Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that Sa'b bin Jaththamah (may Allah be pleased with him) informed me that he heard the Prophet ﷺ being asked about the ruling concerning the families of the polytheists when a night raid is carried out against them and small children and women are also affected by it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: ""They are from among them."" Amr bin Dinar narrated from Zuhri with these additional words: ""They are from among their fathers."" (Meaning, they belong to the same lineage.)"
"1737","It is narrated from Sa'b bin Jaththamah Al-Laithi (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) was asked about the ruling concerning the families of the polytheists when a night raid is carried out against them, and small children and women are also affected by it. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""They are from among them."" Sometimes Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated the words in the hadith as: ""They are from among their fathers."""
"1738","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) asked him: When you lay siege to a city, what do you do? He replied: We send a man dressed in leather clothing towards the city. He (Umar) said: What do you think? If someone throws a stone at him? He replied: Then he will be killed. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Then do not do this. By Him in Whose hand is my soul! I do not like that you conquer a city in exchange for the loss of a single Muslim, even if there are four fighting men in it."
"1739","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that we besieged the fortress of Tustar. Hormuzan surrendered on the order of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), so I brought him to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him). When we reached him, Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Speak. He said: Should I speak like a living (i.e., brave) man or like a dead (i.e., cowardly) man? Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Speak, you have nothing to fear (i.e., say whatever you wish without fear or danger). He said: We and you are inhabitants of Arabia, but Allah has made a distinction between us and you. We used to enslave you, kill you, and seize your wealth, but when Allah sided with you, we became weak. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to me: What do you say? I said: O Commander of the Faithful! I have left behind many enemies and much strength after me. If you kill him, people will lose hope in life and thus their strength (will further) increase. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Should I spare the killer of Bara’ bin Malik and Mujazzah bin Thawr? When I feared that he would kill him, I said: Now there is no justification for his killing; you have said to him, “Speak, you have nothing to fear.” Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Have you taken a bribe from him? I said: By Allah! I have neither taken a bribe nor anything else. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Present another witness besides yourself for what you have testified, otherwise I will punish you. I left from there and met Zubair bin Al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him), and he testified with me. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) did not kill him; later he became Muslim, so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) fixed a stipend for him."
"1740","It is narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that he stated: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) reached Khaybar at night, and whenever the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would come to a people (for attack) at night, he would not attack them until morning. If he heard the call to prayer (adhan), he would not attack, but if they were not among those who prayed, he would attack them at dawn. When morning came, he (ﷺ) mounted, and the Muslims also mounted. The people of the settlement came out with their baskets and containers. When they saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), they said: ""Muhammad (ﷺ) and (his) army."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Allahu Akbar, Khaybar is ruined. When we descend upon a people, then evil is the morning for those who have been warned (of the punishment of Hell)."" Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I was riding behind Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him), and my foot was touching the blessed foot of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."
"1741","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Awn that Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) wrote to him and informed him that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) informed that when the Prophet ﷺ attacked Banu al-Mustaliq, they were completely unaware, with their animals at the place of Muraysi'. The Prophet ﷺ killed their fighting men and took the women and children captive."
"1742","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) burned the properties of Banu Nadir."
"1743","It is narrated from Ibn Shihab that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set fire to the properties of Banu Nadir, so someone said: ""To the chiefs of Banu Lu'ayy (Quraysh), the fire that spread all around in the gardens of Buwairah proved to be a trivial incident."""
"1744","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (ﷺ) had the date palm trees of Banu Nadir cut down and set on fire, and those (gardens) were in a place called Buwaira."
"1745","It is narrated from Ibn Shihab that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set fire to the properties of Banu Nadir, so someone said: ""The fire that spread all around in the gardens of Buwairah proved to be a trivial incident for the chiefs of Banu Lu'ayy (i.e., the leaders of Quraysh)."""
"1746","Usama bin Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered me to attack the area of ""Abna"" in the morning and to burn their (equipment and belongings)."
"1747","Abu Qatadah Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year of the Battle of Hunayn. When we met the enemy, at first the Muslims began to waver. I saw a man from among the polytheists who had overpowered a Muslim. I quickly turned towards him until I came from behind and struck his neck with my sword. Then he turned towards me and squeezed me so hard that I feared for my life. Then death overtook him and he let me go. Later, I met Umar ibn Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him: What is the state of the people now? He said: What Allah had decreed has happened. Then the people (i.e., the Muslims) returned to the battle (i.e., they regrouped). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever kills a disbeliever and has proof of it, then all his belongings will be given to him.” (Abu Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with him, says) I stood up and said: Who will testify for me? Then I sat down. The Prophet ﷺ repeated the same statement, so I stood up and said: Who will testify for me? (No one testified) so I sat down again. The Prophet ﷺ repeated the same statement a third time, so I stood up a third time. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Abu Qatadah, what is it?” Then I narrated the whole incident before him. A man from the people said: O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! He has spoken the truth, and the belongings of the slain man are with me, so please make him pleased with me regarding this. But Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: No, by Allah! We will not do this to one of Allah’s lions who fought for Allah’s sake and then give his belongings to you. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Abu Bakr has spoken the truth. Then he ﷺ gave the belongings to him (Abu Qatadah). Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Prophet ﷺ gave me those belongings, so I sold the armor and bought a garden in Banu Salamah, and this was the first wealth I acquired after accepting Islam. Imam Malik said: “Mukhraf” means a date-palm (tree)."
"1748","It is narrated from Sa’ib bin Yazid (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) wore two coats of mail, one over the other, on the day of the Battle of Uhud."
"1749","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (ﷺ) allotted two shares for a horse and one share for its owner from the spoils of war."
"1750","It is narrated from Yahya bin ‘Abbad bin ‘Abdullah bin Zubair (may Allah be pleased with him) that Zubair bin al-‘Awwam used to take four shares from the spoils of war: one share for himself, two shares for his horse, and one share for the relatives (Dhi al-Qurba). Imam Shafi‘i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: That is, and Allah knows best, by the share of kinship is meant the share of his mother, Safiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her). Sufyan was uncertain whether he had memorized this statement from Hisham by hearing it directly. Sufyan had no doubt that this is a hadith of Hisham through Yahya, nor did he or anyone else who memorized from Hisham have any doubt about it."
"1751","Jubair bin Mut'im (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed the share of close relatives among Banu Hashim and Banu Muttalib, I and Uthman bin Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) came to him. We said: O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! These are our brothers from Banu Hashim; we do not deny their virtue because Allah has placed you among them, but what is your opinion about our brothers from Banu Muttalib that you gave them the share of kinship and left us out or prevented us, even though our relationship to you is the same as theirs? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: ""Banu Hashim and Banu Muttalib are one and the same."" And he interlaced his fingers together."
"1752","In one chain, it is narrated from Jubair bin Mut’im (may Allah be pleased with him) through Zuhri (may Allah have mercy on him). And in another version, it is narrated from Zuhri, from Ibn Musayyib, from Jubair bin Mut’im, from the Prophet ﷺ, with the same meaning as the previous hadith."
"1753","With another chain of narration, a hadith with the same meaning as the previous one is narrated from the Prophet ﷺ through Jubair bin Mut’im (may Allah be pleased with him). Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I mentioned this matter to Mutarrif bin Mazin, that both Yunus and Ibn Ishaq narrated the hadith of Ibn Shihab from Ibn Musayyib. So he said: Ma’mar narrated the hadith to us just as I narrated, and perhaps Ibn Shihab narrated it to both of them at the same time."
"1754","It is narrated from Ali bin Hussain, may Allah have mercy on him, that he reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the same manner. And he added these words: ""May Allah's curse be upon the one who creates division between Banu Hashim and Banu Muttalib."""
"1755","It is narrated from Jubair bin Mut'im (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) distributed the share of kinship among Banu Hashim and Banu Muttalib, and did not give anything from it to any man from Banu Abd Shams or Banu Nawfal."
"1756","Abdur Rahman bin Abi Laila narrated that I met Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) near Ahjar az-Zayt (this is the name of a place in Madinah), so I asked him: ""May my parents be sacrificed for you, what did Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) do regarding the right of khums for you, the Ahl al-Bayt?"" So Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) replied: ""As for Abu Bakr (may Allah have mercy on him), during his time there was no new wealth for khums, and whatever (already existed), he gave it to us in full. And Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) also continued to give us until the wealth of Sus and Ahwaz came to him (or he said: Ahwaz or Fars, Imam Shafi'i is in doubt). Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said that now there is poverty among the Muslims, so if you wish, you may forgo your right and we will spend it to relieve the Muslims' need until more wealth comes to us and we can fulfill your right from it."" Then Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Are you making them covetous of our right?"" So I said to him: ""O Abu al-Fadl! Are we not more deserving to obey the Commander of the Faithful and to relieve the Muslims' poverty?"" Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) passed away before any wealth came to him and he could pay us. Hakam and another narrator said in the hadith of Matar that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""You have a right, but I do not know, when this wealth becomes abundant, is all of it for you? If you wish, I will give you as much as I consider to be your right."" (Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said) We refused except that all of it be given to us, so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) also refused to give it to us."
"1757","It is narrated from Malik bin Aws that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Everyone has a right to this wealth, whether it has been given to him or withheld from him, except for your slaves."""
"1758","It is narrated from Malik bin Aws through Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) in the same manner, and (further) he said: ""If I remain alive, even the shepherd of Sir Himyar will receive his due."""
"1759","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent an army towards Najd, in which Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) was also present. They received a great number of camels as spoils of war. Each warrior's share was 12 or 11 camels, then one camel each was given to them as a reward."
"1760","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated that I was presented before the Prophet (ﷺ) in the year of the Battle of Uhud and I was 14 years old, but he (ﷺ) did not accept me (for participation in the battle). Then I was presented before him in the year of the Battle of Khandaq when I was 15 years old, and he (ﷺ) gave me permission. Nafi‘ (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I narrated this hadith to ‘Umar bin ‘Abdul Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him), and he said: “This is the distinction (limit) between those who fight and the boys.” And he wrote that those who are 15 years old should be given a share of the spoils of war like the fighters, and those who have not reached 15 years should be counted among the boys."
"1761","Malik bin Aws bin Hadthan (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates that I heard Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) while Abbas and Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) also came to him with a dispute regarding the property of the Prophet ﷺ (which Allah had given to him as Fay’). So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “The property of Banu Nadir was given by Allah to His Messenger without any battle, for which the Muslims did not race horses or camels. And this property was specifically for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, apart from the Muslims (i.e., the Companions had no share in it). So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would give his family their annual expenses from it, and whatever remained, he would spend in preparation for jihad in the way of Allah on horses and war equipment. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, then Abu Bakr as-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) acted as guardian over it in the same way as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had done. Then I also acted as guardian over it in the same way as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) had done. Then both of you (Ali and Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both) said to me that I should appoint you as guardians, so I appointed both of you as guardians on the condition that you both would act in it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) had done. Now you both have come to me with a dispute; do you want me to give each of you half and half? Do you both now want a decision from me other than the previous decision? No! By the One by whose command the heavens and the earth stand, I will not make any decision among you except this. If you are unable to manage it, then hand over that property to me, and I will take care of it.” Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said that Sufyan (may Allah have mercy on him) said to me: “I did not hear this hadith from Zuhri (may Allah have mercy on him), and perhaps Amr bin Dinar (may Allah have mercy on him) told me from Zuhri (may Allah have mercy on him).” (Imam Shafi’i, may Allah have mercy on him, said) I asked: “Just as you have narrated the incident (was it exactly like this)?” He replied: “Yes.”"
"1762","Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""If the wealth of Bahrain comes to me, I will give you such and such."" Before the wealth arrived, the Prophet (ﷺ) passed away. Then, when that wealth came to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), he gave me from it. Rabi' (may Allah have mercy on him) said that the remaining part of the hadith, from ""when it came to me..."" onwards, has been narrated to me by no one except al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him)."
"1763","It is narrated from Yazid bin Hurmaz that Najdah wrote a letter to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) asking: Did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) allow women to participate in battles? And was any share (from the spoils of war) fixed for them? Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) replied that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to bring women along in battles; they would treat the wounded, and he (ﷺ) did not allocate any fixed share for them, but he would certainly give them something from the spoils."
"1764","It is narrated from Yazid bin Hurmaz that Najdah wrote a letter to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) asking him some questions. So Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: People say that Ibn Abbas writes to the Haruriyyah, and if I did not fear the punishment for concealing knowledge, I would never have written to him. Najdah had written to him saying: After praise and thanks, tell me, did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) take women along in battle? And did he give them a share (from the spoils of war)? And did he (ﷺ) kill children? When does the orphanhood of an orphan end? And whose is the khums (one-fifth share)? So Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) wrote to him (in reply): You wrote to me and asked me whether the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to take women along in battle? And he (ﷺ) used to take them along, and they would treat the sick, and they would be given something (as a reward) from the spoils, but a separate share was not allocated for them. And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not kill (the disbelievers’) children, so do not kill them either, unless you have the knowledge about a child like Khidr (peace be upon him) had when he killed the child, by which you can distinguish between a believer and a disbeliever, and thus kill the disbeliever and spare the believer. And you wrote asking when the orphanhood of an orphan ends? By the One Who gave me life! Sometimes a man’s beard grows, yet he has neither the sense to take nor to give, but when he begins to speak wisely for his own benefit as people do, then his orphanhood has ended. And you wrote asking me about the khums, so we say that the khums is for us, but our people did not accept that, so we have been patient over it."
"1765","Jarir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that the people of the Bajila tribe were a quarter of the people, so a quarter of the spoils (goods) was distributed to them. For three or four years (the narrator says) I am uncertain about this. They remained like this (i.e., their number remained large). Then I came to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), and with me was a woman from them, so-and-so bint so-and-so. The narrator says he mentioned her name but I do not remember it now. So Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: If I were not a distributor who would be questioned, I would have left you with the wealth that was distributed to you. But now I think you should return (the wealth) to the people."
